+++

# Objective

Reorganize the Java application resources according to their functional responsibilities and extend the default configuration.

# 1. Resource organization

The application resources MUST follow this directory structure:

```text
src/main/resources/
├── defaults.toml
├── messages/
│   └── ...
└── bin/
    ├── windows/
    ├── linux/
    └── macos/
```

Resources MUST NOT reproduce the Java package hierarchy.

## 1.1. Default configuration

Move the existing `defaults.toml` to the root of the resources directory.

Preserve all existing configuration values.

Extend the default configuration with the following section:

```toml
[directories]
host = "iasi-host"
machine = "config-data"
```

These directory names MUST be configurable through the existing configuration mechanism and its established precedence rules.

Remove hardcoded references to these directory names from the Java implementation where applicable.

The default values of `--definitions` and `--machines` MUST remain `.`.

## 1.2. Localized messages

Move all existing localized message resources into the `messages` directory.

Preserve:

- Existing message identifiers.
- Existing translations.
- Existing localization formats.
- Existing language selection and fallback behaviour.

Adapt the message-loading mechanism to the new resource location.

Do not redesign the message management implementation.

## 1.3. Platform resources

Preserve the existing platform-specific resource directories under `bin`.

Windows, Linux and macOS MUST remain independent.

Do not modify the platform-specific scripts as part of this task.

# 2. Host recreation flag

Introduce the `r` flag for host recreation.

- Default value: `true`.
- Description: Allow host component recreation.
- Follow the existing flag activation and deactivation conventions.

Integrate the flag into:

- Default configuration.
- Command-line argument processing.
- Execution context.
- Help information.

The `r` flag MUST be independent of the `f` flag.

- `f`: force recreation of selected virtual machines.
- `r`: authorize recreation of host components.

Host component recreation additionally requires `recreate = true` in the corresponding component TOML.

The default component recreation policy is `false`.

The actual implementation of host component recreation is outside the scope of this task.

# 3. Resource loading

Adapt all affected Java resource-loading mechanisms to the new directory structure.

Resources MUST remain accessible when executing the application from a packaged JAR.

Remove obsolete resources from their previous locations.

Do not maintain duplicate copies or introduce compatibility aliases for the old resource paths.

# 4. Corporate requirements

- Follow the existing structured programming guidelines.
- Follow the corporate Java and Javadoc conventions.
- Reuse existing configuration and argument-processing mechanisms.
- Preserve existing configuration precedence.
- Preserve existing application behaviour.
- Do not refactor unrelated packages.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.

# 5. Validation

Restrict validation to the functionality affected by this task:

- Successful compilation.
- Correct resource packaging.
- Default configuration loading.
- Localized message loading.
- Configurable directory names.
- Host recreation flag processing.
- Updated help information.

Do not execute the complete test suite or corporate Quality Gate.

# 6. Version

Update the application version to `0.2.1`.

# 7. Acceptance criteria

The application loads its default configuration from the resource root and its localized messages from the `messages` directory.

Directory names are configurable.

The host recreation flag is available and enabled by default.

Existing functionality remains unchanged.